<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cherchell</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarea Mauretaniae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cherchell is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Founded as the Phoenician and Punic port of Iol, it was renamed Caesarea by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, who made it the capital of his Mauretanian kingdom in 25 BCE. Under Roman rule it was the capital of the province of Mauretania Caesariensis and one of the largest cities of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and its ruins and museum preserve many fine sculptures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cherchell.jpg" alt="Cherchell" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yves Jalabert · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherchell%27s_fountain_place.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Iol was probably established around 600 BCE, and the oldest remains found there date from the 5th century BCE. In the 3rd century BCE it was fortified and began issuing the first coins of Numidia. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a> it was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a>, king of Numidia, who made it a royal court, and it was the main capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> and II. It was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 33 BCE. In 25 BCE Augustus made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> king of Mauretania and gave him the city as his capital.</p><p>Juba and his wife Cleopatra, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Cleopatra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, rebuilt it on a lavish scale, with a grid of roads, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, an art gallery and a lighthouse modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Pharos of Alexandria</a>. Their son Ptolemy was assassinated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> in 40 CE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> sent legions to complete the conquest of Mauretania. By 44 most resistance had ended and the kingdom was divided into two provinces, one of them Mauretania Caesariensis, governed from Caesarea. The city became a Roman colony and reached a population of over 20,000, possibly 30,000.</p><p>It was the harbour of the Mauretanian Fleet, set up as a permanent force after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> raids in the early 170s, and it had a hippodrome, an amphitheatre, temples and a basilica. Macrinus, a native of the city, became emperor in 217. A Berber revolt sacked the city in 371 and 372, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> ravaged it after 429. Its schools produced the grammarian Priscian. Byzantine forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> brought it back under imperial rule after 533, but it declined. It fell to Umayyad forces in the late 7th century, and by the ninth century much of its defences were beyond repair. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> captured it in 1520, and the French took it in 1840 after a siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cherchell, 89 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, is the seat of Cherchell District in Tipaza Province. Its name is the French transcription of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> word meaning waterfall. It has industries in marble, plaster quarries and iron mines. The local museum displays ancient Greek and Roman antiquities, and the Mosque of the Hundred Columns, now a hospital, contains 89 diorite columns. Many statues found there are in the Museum of Antiquities in Algiers. The writer and film director <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assia-djebar">Assia Djebar</a> was born in Cherchell.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cherchell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital was Caesarea (<b>Cherchell</b>), the former royal city of Juba II, and it was governed by an equestrian procurator with auxiliary troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li></ul></section>
